 ## Two ways to ask
 
-<!-- TODO: `lakeletq query` takes the same question as flags and as one `--sql` text. Say why the
-two are mutually exclusive and why `--store` is a flag in both forms, then show one call in each
-form with the answer it prints. Link the command line. -->
+`lakeletq query` takes the same question two ways, and they are mutually exclusive:
+
+```sh
+% lakeletq query --store ./lakelet-data --from 1700000000000 --to 1700003600000 \
+      --window 5m --agg mean
+1700000000000 41 12.5
+1700000300000 37 12.7
+
+% lakeletq query --store ./lakelet-data --sql \
+      "SELECT mean(value) FROM cpu WHERE host = 'h0'
+         AND ts BETWEEN 1700000000000 AND 1700003600000
+         GROUP BY time(5m), series LIMIT 3"
+cpu,host=h0 1700000000000 41 12.5
+cpu,host=h0 1700000300000 37 12.7
+```
+
+The flags are the shape `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` shipped; the text is this step's, and a call
+that gives both is refused as a caller error, because the text *is* the whole question. `--store`
+is a flag in both forms: a query text names a metric, not a directory.
 
 ## The grammar
 
-<!-- TODO: print the grammar the parser implements — every clause, the five aggregates, the five
-window units — and then the rules that are decisions rather than syntax: the mandatory, half-open
-`ts BETWEEN`; `FROM` naming the metric rather than the series; `AND` only; the mandatory
-`GROUP BY time(<window>)` on the absolute grid and what it does to a range that is not a whole
-number of windows long; `GROUP BY …, series` and what it changes about the answer; `LIMIT`
-bounding series rather than buckets. Link the parser. -->
+```text
+SELECT <agg>(value) FROM <metric> WHERE <pred> [AND <pred>]*
+       GROUP BY time(<window>)[, series] [LIMIT <n>]
+<pred>   ::= <key> = '<value>' | ts BETWEEN <millis> AND <millis>
+<agg>    ::= count | sum | mean | min | max        (case-insensitive, like every keyword)
+<window> ::= <n><unit>, <unit> in ms | s | m | h | d
+```
 
-## Refusals
+Six rules are decisions rather than syntax, and each of them is a sentence somebody had to write
+down:
 
-<!-- TODO: the table a reader of the exit codes opens: one row per way a query is turned down, the
-class it carries (2, 3 or 4) and what it means. Name the refusal variants — the class is a
-property of the refusal and not of the code path that produced it — and say what the sentence a
-caller reads contains: the byte, the word, the floor, the name. -->
+- **the time range is mandatory, and half-open.** `ts BETWEEN a AND b` means `[a, b)`: a record at
+  `a` is in the answer and a record at `b` is the first record of the *next* question. Every range
+  in this store is half-open — [`Range`](../src/engine/query/downsample.rs), the retention cutoff,
+  a segment's stated span — because an inclusive end makes two answers that meet both claim the
+  record they meet on. The range is also **required**: a telemetry query that reads all of time is
+  not a question, it is an accident with a fleet behind it.
+- **`FROM` names the metric, not the series.** A record's series is `metric,tag=value,…`, so
+  `FROM cpu` is every series whose metric is `cpu` and every `WHERE <key> = '<value>'` keeps only
+  the records whose series carries that pair, exactly. A series that carries the metric and *more*
+  tags than the predicates named is selected — the predicates are conditions, not a complete tag
+  set. `ts` is not a tag; it is the time axis, and a predicate on it is refused in a sentence that
+  says so.
+- **`AND` only.** Every predicate has to hold. `OR` is refused rather than answered because it is
+  the one clause whose presence changes what a plan may prune: a record that satisfies either half
+  cannot be ruled out by a predicate that matches one half. `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` is where
+  that argument is made in full.
+- **`GROUP BY time(<window>)` is mandatory.** A query that does not say how wide one bucket is has
+  not said what it wants, and every bucket this store answers is a bucket of the **absolute time
+  grid**: `[n * width, (n + 1) * width)` counted from the Unix epoch, the same alignment a
+  retention cutoff snaps to. A range that is not a whole number of windows long therefore touches
+  one window more than a reader may have counted, and the first and last buckets are partial — they
+  hold what is in the *range*, and nothing that is not.
+- **`GROUP BY …, series` is what makes an answer per series.** Without it, one answer mixes every
+  series the predicates select, which is right for "how busy is the fleet" and wrong for "how busy
+  is this host". With it, the answer is one bucket list per series, ordered by series name, and
+  each line carries the series it is about.
+- **`LIMIT` bounds the number of *series* answered**, so it is written with `GROUP BY …, series`
+  and nowhere else: a limit on one mixed answer names nothing to bound. `LIMIT 0` is refused for
+  the same reason an empty range is — a query that asks for no answer is a mistake, not a small
+  request.
+
+An empty window is **absent**, not zero: a window with no records in the range is not a line, and
+not a `0`, because a store that reported nothing and a store that reported zero are different
+facts. `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` argues that rule where the buckets are computed.
+
+## Asking, and being refused
+
+The answer is one line per bucket on stdout, `start count value`, oldest window first, with the
+series name in front when the query groups by series; `--json` is the same answer as one document.
+The exit code is the part a script branches on, and there is one per class:
+
+| what happened | exit | stdout |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| the question was answered | `0` | the answer |
+| the call was wrong | `2` | nothing |
+| the thing asked about is not there | `3` | nothing |
+| the store refused | `4` | nothing |
+| this deployment is broken | `1` | nothing |
+
+Every caller mistake is answered **before a store is opened**, so a `2` is the script's problem
+wherever the deployment happens to be. The refusal itself is a typed value, and the class is a
+property of the refusal rather than of the code path that produced it:
 
 | refusal | class | what it means |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| TODO | TODO | TODO |
+| `Syntax` | `2` | the text is not a query: a missing, repeated or out-of-order clause, a value that is not quoted, a `'` that never closes (at the byte it opened) |
+| `Aggregate` | `2` | the name after `SELECT` is not one of the five |
+| `Disjunction` | `2` | an `OR`, which this grammar does not have |
+| `Limit` | `2` | `LIMIT` without `series`, or a limit of zero |
+| `Forgot` | `2` | the range reaches below the store's floor; the sentence names the floor and what the store still answers |
+| `Unknown` | `3` | the metric is one this store has never held — a typo must not look like a quiet fleet |
+| `Store` | `4` | a segment or a manifest this build cannot read |
+
+The text form's document carries the counts beside the buckets, which is where a script reads what
+a query cost; the flag form's `--json` is the array it has always been.
 
 ## `EXPLAIN`
 
-<!-- TODO: what `EXPLAIN` is (a statement and not a flag), that it opens no segment, and a key
-table with every key the plan prints: `metric`, `series`, `aggregate`, `from`, `to`, `window`,
-`windows`, `columns`, `segments_opened`, `segments_pruned`, `records`, `limit`. Say which of those
-are the plan's numbers and which are the executed outcome's. Link the plan. -->
+`EXPLAIN` is a statement, not a flag: `EXPLAIN SELECT …` answers with the plan and **opens no
+segment at all**. Every key is a fact the manifest already states:
+
+```text
+metric = cpu
+series = cpu,host=h0
+aggregate = mean
+from = 1700000000000
+to = 1700003600000
+window = 300000
+windows = 12
+columns = series,ts_millis,value
+segments_opened = 2
+segments_pruned = 6
+records = 88
+limit = none
+```
+
+| key | what it is |
+| --- | --- |
+| `metric` | the metric `FROM` named |
+| `series` | the series name the grammar can spell out of the predicates: a *prefix* of every series the answer can cover, not a list of them |
+| `aggregate` | which of the five summarises a window |
+| `from`, `to` | the half-open range, in milliseconds since the epoch |
+| `window` | how wide one bucket is, in milliseconds |
+| `windows` | how many windows the range touches (`MAX_WINDOWS` bounds this, before a store is opened) |
+| `columns` | which columns of a segment the answer is computed from: `series,ts_millis,value` |
+| `segments_opened` | how many segments the plan keeps |
+| `segments_pruned` | how many its stated spans rule out, so nothing is read from them |
+| `records` | how many records the opened segments hold, from the index |
+| `limit` | `none`, or how many series the answer may cover |
+
+`segments_pruned` is a claim about what the query will *not* do, and the executed counts are the
+[outcome's](../src/engine/query/exec.rs): `rows_scanned` is what was actually read (at most what
+`records` states) and `rows_answered` is what landed in a bucket (at most `rows_scanned`). A plan
+that reported a number it did not compute would be worse than no plan.
 
 ## The pipeline
 
-<!-- TODO: the four steps — prune, read, select, answer — and the counts each one produces. Say
-that the buckets come from `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s pass unchanged, and link the executor and
-the binary on top of the library. -->
+```text
+  query text ──▶ lex ──▶ parse ──▶ Query ──▶ plan ──▶ segments to open, and the ones to skip
+                                                  │
+                     the manifest's spans ────────┘
+                                                  ▼
+                                    read ──▶ select ──▶ downsample ──▶ the answer
+                                    the kept   metric +           the window pass of
+                                    segments   predicates         lakelet/4.10
+```
+
+Four steps, in that order, and the interesting one is the first: **pruning**. A segment whose
+stated span does not meet the range holds nothing the query asked about, so it is not opened, and
+the records a query walked is a number the plan chose rather than a number the store imposed.
+Everything after it is deliberately not reimplemented: the buckets come from
+`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s pass, unchanged, so there is one place in this project where a `mean`
+is defined. `cli` is the other end of the same pipeline — [the command line](../src/cli/mod.rs)
+decides about this process (two streams, one exit code, `--json` as a format), and
+[the binary](../src/bin/lakeletq.rs) writes what the library answered and nothing else.
 
 ## What is not here yet
 
